      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "In chronic pancreatitis (CP), a debilitating, progressive and incurable disease, patients' wellbeing is considerably impaired, but the different factors affecting quality of life (QoL), have not been identified yet."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "69 patients with CP were evaluated (M/F 55/14; mean age 46.6 +/- 10.05 years). Different degrees of pancreatic damage were defined using the Cambridge classification; pain intensity and frequency were assessed using pain index. QoL was measured using EORTC QLQ-C30 and the PAN26 questionnaire. Although developed for pancreatic cancer, the C30/PAN26 has been validated for chronic pancreatitis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Digestive symptoms, financial difficulties, fear of future health and general pain scales showed considerable effects of CP on QoL. We observed significant negative correlation between mean QoL scores and pain index in almost all domains (p < 0.001, p < 0.05). Pain intensity affects QoL scales more often than pain frequency. BMI correlated positively with QoL in global health status, altered bowel habits, body image and satisfaction with health care domains (p < 0.01, p < 0.05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Pain index, BMI, Cambridge classification and disease duration are the most important factors adversely affecting QoL in CP. Measurement of QoL is essential in the disease management and improves the knowledge of psychosocial functioning of these patients. and IAP."
        }
      ]},
